## GitHub Pull Request Preferences

### Title Format
- Use clear, descriptive titles (not conventional commits)
- Start with action verb (Integrate, Add, Update, Fix, etc.)
- Keep under 72 characters
- No trailing periods

### Description Structure
- Start with overview paragraph
- Use section titles without markdown headers
- Bold section titles with **
- Use bullet points for lists
- Include technical details but avoid promotional language
- End with related issues/links

### Content Requirements
- Describe what was changed, not just what was added
- Include dependency version changes with before/after
- Mention breaking changes (if any)
- Reference file counts and change statistics
- Include testing status and verification steps

### Review Process
- Ensure all changes are backward compatible
- Verify CI/CD passes
- Check that dependencies are up-to-date
- Confirm no linting errors remain
- Test in development environment

### Branch Management
- Use descriptive branch names (feature/, fix/, refactor/)
- Keep branches focused on single feature/area
- Delete branches after merge
- Rebase before merging (avoid merge commits)

### Commit Strategy
- Multiple focused commits within PR
- Clear commit messages for each change
- Logical grouping of related changes
- Amend commits to fix issues rather than new commits
